1
00:00:00,060 --> 00:00:03,850
This Scandinavian line looks quiet until the queen gets chased.

2
00:00:04,450 --> 00:00:08,310
This is the Scandinavian Portuguese Trap in the Scandinavian Defense.

3
00:00:08,840 --> 00:00:13,350
The first moves look completely normal. Develop, challenge the center, and wait.

4
00:00:13,660 --> 00:00:18,120
Both sides keep playing natural moves, and the position starts to hide the real idea.

5
00:00:18,430 --> 00:00:23,470
Now the pieces point toward the target. One loose defender is all the trap needs.

6
00:00:23,780 --> 00:00:28,890
The bait appears. It looks like free material, but it is the move the trap was waiting for.

7
00:00:29,200 --> 00:00:34,520
The reply is forcing. The attacker does not need to calculate ten moves, only the next check.

8
00:00:34,830 --> 00:00:39,290
The point is simple: Black has forced the bishop back while gaining tempi in the center.

9
00:00:39,600 --> 00:00:45,330
The king has fewer choices than it seems, and every escape square is covered by a piece or a pawn.

10
00:00:45,640 --> 00:00:49,430
And now Black has forced the bishop back while gaining tempi in the center.

11
00:00:50,430 --> 00:00:55,470
That is why the tempting move fails: the position was already built around the final tactic.

12
00:00:55,780 --> 00:01:00,980
Save the pattern. In a real game, spotting the idea one move earlier is the whole point.

13
00:01:01,290 --> 00:01:03,130
Would you see it before taking the bait?

